rently small (200 chips), and the previous round (Flop) went Check-Check, suggesting the opponent does not have a monster hand. Being out of position with a strong draw, checking is the best move. It gives me a chance to see the river card for free if the opponent checks back. If I bet, I risk being raised and forced to fold my equity or play a large pot with only a draw. Since I am leading the cumulative score, a low-variance play like checking is preferred.\n\nReason: I possess a strong draw (Open-Ended Straight Draw) with 8 outs (5s and 10s) and potential overcard outs. Checking is the optimal strategy to minimize risk and pot size while trying to realize my hand's equity on the river.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
